    40	---
    41	
    42	## Abstract (≤ 200 words)
    43	
    44	Bayesian Optimization (BO) practice reduces many sequential decision points
    45	(refit surrogate, switch acquisition, expand region, or stop) to a single fixed
    46	acquisition function. We re-frame these as an explicit meta-decision: at every
    47	state `s_t`, choose an action `a_t ∈ A` that updates the BO process. On a
    48	5-family BOCoDe panel × 20 paired seeds we measure five things:
    49	
    50	1. **Decision landscape** — we identify three regimes (Flat, Heavy-tailed,
    51	   Frequent-opportunity) using an `adaptive-value` metric on counterfactual
    52	   oracle labels.
    53	2. **Affine invariance (partial)** — gold_probs **median rank correlation is
    54	   Spearman ρ = 1.0000 across all 17 (problem, phase) buckets** under
    55	   y → scale * y + shift. The predeclared joint Gate 5 (which also requires
    56	   JS ≤ 0.02) **fails on 2/17 Hartmann buckets** due to GP-fit numerical
    57	   fragility in the rollout's surrogate acquisition proposal. Per-record
    58	   rank invariance is not established.
    59	3. **LLM as System-1** — a deepseek-chat LLM used at every decision beats
    60	   AlwaysSurrogate by **+17.54 best_y** on Rastrigin (paired-t **p=0.0058**,
    61	   Student-t df=n-1, n=10; it is detectably worse on Branin, Hartmann and
    62	   SixHumpCamel, §6.1) and exhibits a **global-to-local temporal action
    63	   pattern** *associated with* its advantage. A predeclared deterministic
    64	   two-phase schedule realizing that pattern **reproduces and exceeds the
    65	   LLM's closed-loop value** (CI-excluding-zero wins on 3/5 families, never
    66	   loses; §6.2/§6.5) — a two-phase schedule suffices to match or exceed the
    67	   LLM's value on this panel, so LLM-specific per-step reasoning is not
    68	   needed to explain it (a step-level causal decomposition remains open,
    69	   §7.9). A simple `dim≥5` router beats
    70	   Surrogate on the BOCoDe panel (p=0.0120) but does NOT transfer to
    71	   IOH/BBOB (p=0.1376, negative result).
    72	4. **Cost frontier (10 seeds, closed loop)** — with a frozen RF System-1
    73	   deployed through full BO trajectories, the always-LLM policy never beats
    74	   System-1-only with a CI excluding zero and is detectably worse on Ackley,
    75	   Hartmann, and SixHumpCamel; no confidence-threshold or random-rate routing
    76	   arm earns its cost. A deterministic two-phase schedule dominates the LLM
    77	   itself on 3/5 families — the frontier's degeneracy is a property of the
    78	   System-2 decisions, formalized by a routing-dominance proposition.
    79	5. **Distilling the teacher (decision-level)** — single-roll counterfactual
    80	   labels are noise-dominated (per-re-roll pair flip 0.40 above-median), yet
    81	   RLCD fine-tuning of a 0.4B typed-decision model lifts decision-level
    82	   pairwise ranking from 0.40 (zero-shot) to 0.87 (BT) / 0.80 (proper-score,
    83	   the only calibrated arm), and transfers cross-family at decision level
    84	   (+0.140 and +0.188 on two independent seed streams, 5/5 families each;
    85	   GearTrain engineering probe holds) — but both fine-tuned arms collapse
    86	   to a constant policy in closed loop (§4.7/§5.6).
    87	
    88	Our contribution is a 7-component framework (backend/state/action/utility/
    89	rollout/dataset/system-1+router) with **423 unit tests**, four formal
    90	results in `docs/THEORY.md` (P1/P2 verified numerically through the live
    91	code path; P3/L1 tied empirically), and run artifacts for every reported
    92	experiment. Cross-family transfer is partial and heterogeneous: the RF
    93	System-1 has lower one-step decision regret than the real
    94	state-conditional rule policy on 3/5 families (§4), and an RLCD
    95	fine-tuned model transfers at decision level (+0.140, analysis-rerun;
    96	§4.7); closed-loop and full engineering transfer are not demonstrated,
    97	and within-function dimension transfer is a clean negative.
    98	
    99	---
   100	
   101	## §1 Introduction
   102	
   103	- BO is usually presented as `x → f(x) → x*`, hiding the **sequential
   104	  control decisions** the practitioner makes between sample acquisitions.
   105	- We re-frame as `s_t → a_t → backend → s_{t+1}` — a meta-policy π(a|s).
   106	- Three RQs: Learnability (RQ1), Generalization (RQ2), Selective Reasoning (RQ3).
   107	- **Status**: RQ1 partially settled (Gate 3 adjusted_lift 1.294 CI [0.848, 1.954] at 20 seeds;
   108	  Gate 4 stop-gain 5/5; **Gate 5 partial — median Spearman = 1.0 but predeclared joint
   109	  fails on 2/17 buckets; Gate 2 still fails**); the teacher itself is noise-dominated
   110	  (single-roll pair flip 0.40 above-median) yet distillable at decision level
   111	  (RLCD fine-tuning of Laya: 0.40 → 0.87 pairwise; closed loop collapses to a
   112	  constant policy, §5.6); **RQ2 partially answered** (5-fold
   113	  leave-one-family-out CV: cross-family RF wins vs the real state-conditional
   114	  RulePolicy on **3/5 BOCoDe families** (Branin, Ackley, Rastrigin; seed-block
   115	  95% CI upper < 0); aggregate Cross − Rule = -0.065; loses to a constant
   116	  `local_exploitation` policy (AlwaysLocal) on aggregate; an RLCD fine-tuned
   117	  model transfers at decision level, +0.140 / +0.188 on two independent
   118	  seed streams, 5/5 families each — family-level locking still open, §4.7); RQ3 has one strong
   119	  pilot (LLM > Surrogate on Rastrigin, p=0.0058), one negative pilot
   120	  (router does not transfer to IOH, p=0.1376), and a closed-loop 10-seed
   121	  cost frontier (450 paired trajectories) in which a frozen RF System-1 with
   122	  zero LLM calls beats or ties every LLM-calling arm on all five families;
   123	  a predeclared deterministic two-phase schedule reproduces and exceeds the
   124	  LLM's closed-loop value (§6.2/§6.5).
   125	  Within-function dimension transfer (RQ2.2) is a clean negative: the RQ2.1
   126	  signal is a family signal, not a dimension signal.
   127	- This paper presents the framework + RQ1 + RQ2 partial pilot + RQ3 pilots;
   128	  full RQ2 transfer with engineering families and a crossed (family × dimension)
   129	  design is deferred.
   130	
   131	## §2 Background & Related Work
   132	
   133	- BO and BoTorch (briefly).
   134	- **Agentic BO (Sara/lenz, Brunzema et al. 2026)** — nearest neighbor: open
   135	  command action space (probe/reconfigure/propose/evaluate/stop) over a
   136	  BoTorch backend, full (trials, config, instructions, deliberation-history)
   137	  state, no learned policy, token cost reported but not optimized. Our
   138	  closed 8-action space over a compressed 15-scalar process state is the
   139	  opposite trade: expressiveness for measurability — closure enables
   140	  exhaustive per-decision counterfactual labels over our predefined menu
   141	  (and with them the predeclared gates and the noninferiority cost
   142	  frontier); it does not rule out counterfactual comparisons elsewhere.
   143	  Full state/action design spectrum:
   144	  `docs/ref_papers/state_action_design_survey.md`.
   145	- Meta-learning for algorithm selection (Biedenkapp et al. on algorithm
   146	  configuration; TempoRL's skip-actions are the anchor for our zero-cost
   147	  actions and when-to-route; our analogue is per-trajectory action selection).
   148	- LLM-as-decision-maker literature (LLAMBO ICLR 2024 — prompt-state with the
   149	  full evaluation history, action = next x; GPTOpt 2025 — fine-tuned
   150	  LLM-as-optimizer; both act in x-space, we act at the strategy layer).
   151	- Decision-theoretic and classical-BO foundations (one paragraph in the
   152	  paper, full map in `docs/THEORY.md` "Relation to classical theory"):
   153	  the GP posterior is the classical sufficient statistic our 15-scalar
   154	  state projects; EI/GP-UCB are closed-form one-step decision rules — our
   155	  utility-plus-menu lifts that structure to the strategy level where no
   156	  closed form exists; GP-Hedge (UAI 2011) and its portfolio successors
   157	  allocate among rules by online bandits, and LMABO (ICLR 2026) delegates
   158	  that allocation to a zero-shot LLM — our P2 is the utility-unit Value of
   159	  Information (Howard 1966 / Lindley 1956 lineage; entropy-based
   160	  acquisitions ES/PES/MES are the nats version) and P3 gives the
   161	  degenerate case the portfolio line assumes away (per-state advantage
   162	  ≤ 0 ⇒ no allocation helps), which our cost frontier measures.
   163	
   164	## §3 The v3 Framework
   165	
   225	
   226	### §3.7 System-1 + Router
   227	
   228	`LayaPolicy` (typed-decision adapter) and `LLMSystem1Policy` (DeepSeek-chat
   229	API or mock). `HybridPolicy` wraps a System-1 + confidence-based router that
   230	falls back to `System2LLMDeepSeek`.
   231	
   232	### §3.8 Theoretical results (full statements + proofs: `docs/THEORY.md`)
   233	
   234	Four short results formalize claims the experiments measure:
   235	
   236	- **P1 (affine invariance, exact).** Under `y → αy + β` (α > 0) with the
   237	  scale `σ_opt = std(y_init)` recomputed, every utility label is unchanged
   238	  (β cancels in the Δopt difference; α cancels against the scale), so
   239	  gold_probs and all rank statistics are invariant by construction. This
   240	  makes Gate 5's Spearman = 1.0 *necessary*, and localizes the 2/17 JS
   241	  breaches to GP-fit numerics rather than the supervision signal. Verified
   242	  through the actual `compute_utility` code path on 5,304 rollout branches
   243	  (max relative error 1.4e-12; `scripts/verify_theory_propositions.py`).
   244	- **P2 (`adaptive-value` = tight value of state-conditionality).**
   245	  `D = E[max_a U] − max_a E[U] ≥ 0` equals the one-step regret of the best
   246	  constant action against per-state oracle selection and tightly bounds the
   247	  gain of *any* state-conditional policy over the best constant action —
   248	  **scope: this fixed 8-action menu, the sampled states, and the rollout
   249	  utility**; it does not bound closed-loop terminal value or gains from an
   250	  expanded action menu.
   251	  The Flat families (SixHumpCamel D = 0.012 at 20 seeds) admit no
   252	  state-conditional gain by bound, which is why the RF probe finds no
   253	  signal there. Recomputed at 20 seeds: Hartmann 0.710 > Ackley 0.332 >
   254	  Rastrigin 0.262 > Branin 0.040 > SixHumpCamel 0.012.
   255	- **P3 (routing degeneracy).** If the System-2's per-state advantage
   256	  `A(s) = Q(s, a₂(s)) − Q(s, a₁(s)) ≤ 0` a.s., *every* router — oracle or
   257	  learned — has non-positive value over never routing. The 10-seed sweep
   258	  (0/40 arms beat `rf` with CI excluding zero) is the empirical signature
   259	  of `A ≤ 0`, so the routing failure is a property of the LLM's decisions,
   260	  not of the routing heuristic.
   261	- **L1 (exact imitation of axis-aligned rules).** A random forest whose
   262	  features contain an axis-aligned threshold rule's inputs reproduces the
   263	  rule exactly under noiseless labels with no feature aliasing — explaining
   264	  RQ2.2's `rf − rule_full = +0.0000` and the inherited losses to
   265	  Surrogate/Local.
   266	
