   164	## L1. Random forests can represent axis-aligned threshold rules; exact imitation is then an empirical observation
   165	
   166	**Lemma 1 (representability).** Let the target rule `r(s)` be a function of
   167	a subset of axis-aligned features (e.g., `stagnation_steps`,
   168	remaining-budget ratio). Suppose training pairs `(features(sᵢ), r(sᵢ))`
   169	are noiseless and cover the rule's decision cells, and there is **no
   170	aliasing**: equal feature vectors imply equal rule actions. Then there
   171	EXIST CART trees (depth ≥ number of thresholds, one regressor per action
   172	on indicator targets) whose ensemble reproduces `r` exactly:
   173	`argmax_a f̂_a(features(s)) = r(s)` on covered states.
   174	
   175	**Scope caveat (added per codex round 5).** This is a representability
   176	statement, not a guarantee about the TRAINED forest: CART fitting with
   177	random subsetting may grow trees that split elsewhere, and coverage plus
   178	no-aliasing do not by themselves ensure the correct partitions dominate
   179	the ensemble average. The claim that a fitted forest DOES recover the
   180	rule exactly is an empirical observation (below), for which the lemma
   181	supplies the explanation of possibility.
   182	
   183	**Proof sketch.** Each CART tree partitions the feature space into
   184	axis-aligned rectangles; the rule's cells are a partition of this kind, so
   185	some tree realizations isolate them. With noiseless indicator targets and
   186	sufficient leaf samples, per-action regressors CAN converge to the
   187	indicator of the rule picking that action on every covered cell, making
   188	the argmax unique for those realizations. ∎
   189	
   190	**Empirical tie.** `RuleFullBudget` decides on
   191	(`stagnation_steps ≥ 3` with budget, low-budget threshold 0) — both
   192	features are in the 15-dim RF feature vector. EXP-RQ2.2 trained on
   193	exactly these labels and measured `rf − rule_full = +0.0000`
   194	(CI [+0.0000, +0.0000]) on the held-out dimension at both 3 seeds and —
   195	after today's extension — 10 seeds, on all three functions. The lemma
   196	also predicts the RF *inherits the rule's losses*: Surrogate and Local
   197	beat `rf` on every function, now with CI excluding zero vs Surrogate on
   198	all three (f6 +0.128, f8 +0.244, f15 +0.225) at 10 seeds. Perfect
   199	imitation of a weak rule is a perfect reproduction of its weakness —
   200	which is the mechanism behind the RQ2.2 negative result.
