   228	## Relation to classical theory (added 2026-09-28)
   229	
   230	The propositions are not built from scratch; each is a strategy-level
   231	counterpart of a classical result. Making the lineage explicit (also the
   232	related-work framing in the outline §2):
   233	
   234	- **P2 (adaptive-value) is the decision-theoretic counterpart of the
   235	  classical Value of Information.** Decision theory has long measured
   236	  "what is knowing more worth": expected value of information (Howard,
   237	  1966, *Information Value Theory*; Raiffa & Schlaifer, 1961) and expected
   238	  information gain in Bayesian experimental design (Lindley, 1956).
   239	  Information-theoretic BO acquisitions are a related but DIFFERENT
   240	  functional, not a unit conversion: Entropy Search (Hennig & Schuler,
   241	  2012) and Predictive Entropy Search (Hernández-Lobato et al., 2014)
   242	  value a candidate point by posterior entropy reduction about the
   243	  optimum's LOCATION, while Max-Value Entropy Search (Wang & Jegelka,
   244	  2017) targets its VALUE. Our `D` is instead a perfect-information
   245	  value measured in utility units about the STATE's oracle label:
   246	  `E_s[max_a U(a,s)] − max_a E_s[U(a,s)]` is the most any policy could
   247	  gain from perfectly knowing which state it is in (the
   248	  Howard-style expected value of revealing the state identity). The
   249	  analogy, not an identity: both ask what knowing more is worth, in
   250	  different currencies about different unknowns. They compose
   251	  empirically: `D ≈ 0` on the flat families means no state-revealing
   252	  information can pay for itself in utility — consistent with richer
   253	  state observation (branch `exp/lenz-state-jev-presentation`, E1/E2)
   254	  buying nothing there.
   255	- **The compressed state is a projection of the classical sufficient
   256	  statistic — with the sufficiency model-conditional and the claim
   257	  task-scoped.** Under the GP model the posterior `p(f | D_t)` is a
   258	  sufficient summary of the trial log; process decisions additionally
   259	  read budget, configuration, and execution state, which are not
   260	  functions of the posterior. Our 15-scalar state is a
   261	  fixed-dimensional projection of `(D_t, c_t)`. The E1 negative result
   262	  (trajectory features add nothing over the projection on 5/5 families)
   263	  shows the projection is near-lossless FOR PREDICTING THE ACTION LABEL
   264	  under the tested feature families — it does not establish losslessness
   265	  in general.
   266	- **The utility-plus-menu design lifts acquisition functions to the
   267	  strategy level.** EI (Mockus, 1978) and GP-UCB (Srinivas et al., 2010)
   268	  are closed-form one-step decision rules derived from the posterior; our
   269	  8-action menu plus hand-specified utility is the same one-step-greedy
   270	  structure at the level of *which optimizer action*, where no closed-form
   271	  rule exists — which is precisely why supervision is needed.
   272	- **P3 stands in an analogy relation to the portfolio/bandit line.**
   273	  GP-Hedge (Hoffman, Brochu & Freitas, UAI 2011) allocates among
   274	  acquisition functions by exponential weights, rewarding EVERY arm each
   275	  round with the updated GP posterior mean at that arm's nominated point
   276	  (a full-information reward, not the realized gain of the chosen arm);
   277	  Shahriari et al.'s Entropy Search Portfolio and Vasconcelos et al.
   278	  (2022) continue the line; LMABO (ICLR 2026) delegates the portfolio
   279	  choice to a zero-shot LLM. That line assumes the portfolio contains an
   280	  arm worth switching to. P3 is the analogous degenerate condition in our
   281	  two-arm routing model — when the expensive arm's per-state advantage is
   282	  ≤ 0, no allocation can extract positive value. The analogy is not an
   283	  equivalence: our sweep measures routing outcomes, not the per-state
   284	  premise itself (limitation 9 of the outline), and our menu (stop, refit,
   285	  sampling strategies) has no per-arm nominated point, so GP-Hedge's
   286	  reward is not directly defined over it.
   287	- **L1 is a representability statement plus an empirical observation.**
   288	  Axis-aligned regression trees CAN represent an axis-aligned threshold
   289	  rule exactly; under noiseless labels, no feature aliasing, and
   290	  sufficient per-leaf samples, a fitted forest can recover the partition
   291	  — but coverage and no-aliasing alone do not GUARANTEE that the trained
   292	  ensemble learns it or that correct trees dominate the average. The
   293	  RQ2.2 observation (`rf − rule_full = +0.0000` on all functions) is the
   294	  empirical realization; the proposition is the representability part.
   295	
